Experiment: Study of Network Commands in Linux and Windows

Aim
To study and practice various network commands used in Linux and Windows operating systems to configure, monitor, and troubleshoot network connectivity.

Algorithm
1. Start the computer and open the Command Prompt in Windows or Terminal in Linux.
2. Execute the ipconfig command in Windows or ifconfig / ip addr in Linux to display IP address information.
3. Use the ping command to check connectivity between computers.
4. Execute ipconfig /all in Windows or ip route in Linux to view network configuration and routing information.
5. Use tracert in Windows or traceroute in Linux to trace the path to a destination.
6. Execute netstat to display active network connections and listening ports.
7. Use nslookup to find the IP address associated with a domain name.
8. Observe and record the output of each command.
9. Stop the execution.

Commands and Sample Output
1. Windows Commands
Command	Purpose	Sample output
ipconfig	Displays IP configuration	IPv4 Address: 192.168.1.10
ipconfig /all	Displays detailed network settings	Physical Address, DNS Server, DHCP
ping google.com	Checks connectivity	Reply from destination
tracert google.com	Traces the route	List of intermediate hops
netstat -an	Displays connections and ports	TCP connection details
nslookup google.com	Resolves a domain name	DNS server and IP address
arp -a	Displays ARP cache	IP and physical address mappings

2. Linux Commands
Command	Purpose	Sample output
ip addr	Displays IP addresses	inet 192.168.1.10/24
ip route	Displays routing table	default via 192.168.1.1
ping -c 4 google.com	Sends four ping requests	4 packets transmitted
traceroute google.com	Traces the network route	Hop-by-hop details
ss -tuln	Displays listening ports	TCP/UDP port information
nslookup google.com	Resolves a domain name	DNS response
ip neigh	Displays neighbor cache	IP and MAC address mappings
